<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joshua</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yehoshua, Joshua son of Nun, Yusha bin Nun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joshua, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> name Yehoshua means '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a> is salvation', was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>' assistant in Exodus and Numbers and, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-joshua">Book of Joshua</a>, his successor as leader of the Israelite tribes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> names him as one of the twelve spies sent into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, and says he led the conquest of the land, including the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jericho">Jericho</a>, and allocated it to the tribes. It gives his age at death as 110. Muslims honour him as Yusha bin Nun, understood to be Moses' attendant in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>. Mainstream scholarship holds that the Book of Joshua is a theological narrative compiled centuries after the events it describes, and that archaeology largely contradicts its account.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joshua.jpg" alt="Joshua" width="250" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joshua._Russian_orthodox_icon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
